  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1223 AM CDT Thu May 14 2026\n\nUnseasonably hot temperatures will continue across West Central \nTexas on Friday. Southwest winds will increase to 10 to 20 mph, with \nhigher gusts, while afternoon highs top out in the upper 90s to 103 \nacross our northwest counties, with low to mid 90s elsewhere. \nAlthough an isolated shower or thunderstorm is possible along the \ndryline Friday afternoon in our western counties, mainly dry \nconditions are forecast to prevail. As we head into the weekend, a \nbroad upper level trough will develop across the western CONUS, \nplacing West Central Texas in west to southwest flow aloft. The \ndryline will advance to near our western border each afternoon, but \noverall expect the cap to suppress any convection. Dry conditions \nare forecast on Saturday, with a low (15-20%) chance of showers and \nthunderstorms on Sunday afternoon and evening. Highs this weekend \nwill continue to be above seasonal normals. Temperatures on Saturday \nwill again be hottest in our northwest counties where highs will be \nin the upper 90s to near 104 and in the low to mid 90s elsewhere. \nHighs on Sunday will be a bit cooler, in the mid 80s to lower 90s.\n\nThe aforementioned upper level trough will then approach West \nCentral Texas early next week. With the added upper level support \nfor this system, a better chance of showers and thunderstorms is \nexpected both Tuesday and Wednesday afternoon and evening. Rain \nchances remain in the low to medium (20-50%) range. Highs early next \nweek will be in the mid 80s to mid 90s, with the hottest \ntemperatures continuing to occur across our northwest counties.",
